    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Jose Caballero** anchor our lineup as the primary offensive catalysts. **Tyler Soderstrom** sits at a 76.91 efficiency score, benefiting immensely from an elite matchup against Johnson (5.29 xERA) and an ideal cleanup spot in the order. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** offers a 72.32 score; while his spot in the seven-hole suppresses his ceiling slightly, he carries a massive platoon advantage against the lefty Hudson and profiles as our premier threat for stolen bases given the soft delivery of the opposing starter. **Josh Naylor** rounds out the top tier with a 66.95 score, utilizing a strong R/R platoon edge against Baz to keep our counting stats flowing despite the pitcher's respectable 4.35 xERA.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Travis Bazzana**, who fell just short of the 40.0 floor with a 34.68 score. While his lead-off role usually warrants consideration, the heavy negative impact of facing Drohan\u2014a lefty-on-lefty nightmare\u2014and the high-skill 3.32 xERA rendered him a liability today. Conversely, **Brice Turang** and **Cole Young** are operating as marginal starts at 48.84 and 48.79 respectively; both are rostered primarily for their stability and high-floor profiles in a landscape where we cannot afford to burn game caps on high-risk alternatives. **Henry Bolte** (58.30) remains a solid, if unspectacular, inclusion in the outfield, though his eighth-order placement limits his immediate run-scoring potential. \n\nWe are monitoring no severe weather risks, though the reliance on **Cole Young** against the stingy Baz (4.35 xERA) requires caution. With **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino** currently unavailable due to scheduling or injury, our depth is being tested. We expect **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Jose Caballero** to provide the necessary spark to keep our 5x5 metrics\u2014specifically stolen bases\u2014within striking distance of the leaders as we navigate this mid-week stretch.",
